---
/**
 * M19 · Scroll — how the page comes to rest when the visitor scrolls.
 *
 * ── WHAT KIND OF MODULE THIS IS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Site-wide plumbing, not a visual section — the same shape as M17 Meta:
 *   · it renders NOTHING;
 *   · it never appears in `page-order.ts`;
 *   · it is included ONCE, by `Base.astro`;
 *   · it never imports from another module, and no module imports it.
 * Delete this folder and the one line in Base, and the site is unchanged.
 *
 * ── REUSING IT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Drop the folder into any Astro project, render <Scroll /> once in the
 * layout, and set `content.ts`. It has no dependency on this site: no token,
 * no primitive, no other module — it reads `--ease` from wherever it lands and
 * falls back to its own curve if that token does not exist.
 *
 * ⚠️ OFF UNTIL THE CLIENT SAYS OTHERWISE (`content.ts → enabled`), and gated
 * TWICE on purpose:
 *   1. `Base.astro` does not RENDER this component while it is disabled, which
 *      is what keeps its script out of the built page entirely. A conditional
 *      around a <script> *tag* does not do that — Astro hoists and bundles
 *      every script in a component that renders, so wrapping the tag still
 *      shipped 259 bytes and two extra requests to every page (measured).
 *   2. The dynamic import below, so even where the component does render the
 *      engine is only fetched when it is actually switched on.
 */
---

<script>
  import { scroll, scrollAttrs } from './content';

  if (scroll.enabled) {
    const root = document.documentElement;
    for (const [name, value] of Object.entries(scrollAttrs())) root.setAttribute(name, value);
    void import('./scroll');
  }
</script>
